#{[17592186649901 :question/worked-solution "Kate's commission is $2%$ of the price of the house. In this case, the price of the house is $\\$60,000.$ So, to calculate how much Kate earns in commission, we need to find $2%$ of $\\$60,000.$\n\nTo do this, we first write $2%$ as a decimal:\n\n$$\n2%=2÷100=0.02\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $60,000:$\n\n$$\n0.02\\times 60,000=1,200\n$$\n\nTherefore, Kate earns $\\$1,200$ in commission."] [17592186649902 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{7}{4}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{4}=\\frac{7\\times 25}{4\\times 25}=\\frac{175}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{175}{100}=175%\n$$"] [17592186649905 :question/problem "After working some extra hours, Helena received $10%$ of her usual salary as overtime pay. What is $10%$ expressed as a fraction?"] [17592186649905 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign ($10$) as a fraction with denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{100}=\\frac{10÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $10%=\\frac{1}{10}.$"] [17592186649907 :question/problem "On World Book Day, a bookstore has a $22%$ discount on all its books. What fraction is equivalent to this percentage?"] [17592186649907 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign $(22)$ as a fraction with denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{22}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{22}{100}=\\frac{22÷2}{100÷2}=\\frac{11}{50}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $22%=\\frac{11}{50}.$"] [17592186650935 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the battery charge is given by the function\n\n$$\nC(t)=95-3.5t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ represents the number of hours after use begins.  \n\nWe substitute $t=8$ into the charge function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(8) & =95-3.5(8) \\\\  & =95-28 \\\\  & =67\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the charge will be $67%$ after $8$ hours."] [17592186651554 :question/problem "Saif bought a barbecue set on sale for $\\$182.$ If the markdown was $35%,$ what was the original price of the set?"] [17592186651554 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percent paid}\\times \\text{original price}=\\text{final price}.\n$$\n\nThe markdown was $35%,$ so the percent that Saif paid was\n\n$$\n100%-35%=65%.\n$$\n\nExpressing $65%$ as a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n65%=65÷100=0.65\\,.\n$$\n\nWe also know that the final price was $\\$182.$ So, if $p$ was the original price, then we have the following equation which we can solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.65p & =182 \\\\ p & =\\frac{182}{0.65} \\\\ p & =280\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original price of the barbecue set was $\\$280.$"] [17592186651559 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percent markdown}=\\frac{\\text{amount of markdown}}{\\text{original price}}.\n$$\n\nThe original price is $45$ dollars. The amount of markdown is $45-31.50=13.50$ dollars. Substituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{percent markdown} & =\\frac{13.5}{45} \\\\  & =\\frac{13.5÷4.5}{45÷4.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10} \\\\  & =3÷10 \\\\  & =0.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert the result to a percent:\n\n$$\n0.3=0.3\\times 100%=30%\n$$\n\nTherefore, the percent markdown is $30%.$"] [17592186651561 :question/problem "John decides to visit his parents and buys a plane ticket. Due to excess baggage, the cost of the ticket increases by $5%$ so John pays a total of $\\$430.50.$ What was the cost of the ticket before the excess baggage additional cost?"] [17592186651561 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percent paid}\\times \\text{original price}=\\text{final price}.\n$$\n\nThe increase was $5%,$ so the percent that John pays was\n\n$$\n100%+5%=105%.\n$$\n\nExpressing $105%$ as a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n105%=105÷100=1.05\\,.\n$$\n\nWe also know that the final price was $\\$430.50.$ So, if $p$ was the original price, then we have the following equation, which we can solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.05p & =430.50 \\\\ p & =\\frac{430.50}{1.05} \\\\ p & =410\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original price of the ticket before the additional cost was $\\$410.00.$"] [17592186651563 :question/problem "Aiden sells salt beef bagels. Due to their popularity, he increases his prices by $16%$ to pay for better ingredients. If a bagel now costs $8.70,$ what was the price of a bagel before the increase?"] [17592186651563 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percent paid}\\times \\text{original price}=\\text{final price}.\n$$\n\nThe increase was $16%,$ so the percent that Aiden sells for is\n\n$$\n100%+16%=116%.\n$$\n\nExpressing $116%$ as a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n116%=116÷100=1.16\\,.\n$$\n\nWe also know that the final price was $\\$8.70.$ So, if $p$ was the original price, then we have the following equation which we can solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.16p & =8.7 \\\\ p & =\\frac{8.7}{1.16} \\\\ p & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original price of the bagel, before the increase, was $\\$7.50.$"] [17592186651564 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percent markdown}=\\frac{\\text{amount of markdown}}{\\text{original price}}.\n$$\n\nThe original price is $10$ dollars. The amount of markdown is $2$ dollars. Substituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{percent markdown} & =\\frac{2}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2÷2}{10÷2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =1÷5 \\\\  & =0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert the result to a percent:\n\n$$\n0.2=0.2\\times 100%=20%\n$$\n\nTherefore, the percent markdown is $20%.$"] [17592186651569 :question/problem "Neve bought an insurance policy for $\\$4,221$ after a rebate. If the markdown was $37%,$ what was the original price of the policy?"] [17592186651569 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percent paid}\\times \\text{original price}=\\text{final price}.\n$$\n\nThe markdown was $37%,$ so the percent that Neve paid was\n\n$$\n100%-37%=63%.\n$$\n\nExpressing $63%$ as a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n63%=63÷100=0.63\\,.\n$$\n\nWe also know that the final price was $\\$4,221.$ So, if $p$ was the original price, then we have the following equation which we can solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.63p & =4,221 \\\\ p & =\\frac{4,221}{0.63} \\\\ p & =6,700\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original price of the policy was $\\$6,700.$"] [17592186651570 :question/problem "A spate of burglaries has lowered the house prices by $9%.$ If Saul's house is now worth $\\$100,100,$ what was the original price of the house?"] [17592186651570 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percent paid}\\times \\text{original price}=\\text{final price}.\n$$\n\nThe markdown was $9%,$ so the percent that Georgie paid was\n\n$$\n100%-9%=91%.\n$$\n\nExpressing $91%$ as a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n91%=91÷100=0.91\\,.\n$$\n\nWe also know that the final price was $\\$100,100.$ So, if $p$ was the original price, then we have the following equation which we can solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.91p & =100,100 \\\\ p & =\\frac{100,100}{0.91} \\\\ p & =110,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original price of the house was $\\$110,000.$"] [17592186651574 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percent markup}=\\frac{\\text{amount of markup}}{\\text{original price}}.\n$$\n\nThe original price is $35$ dollars. The amount of markup is $1.40$ dollars. Substituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{percent markup} & =\\frac{1.40}{35} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.4÷1.4}{35÷1.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25} \\\\  & =1÷25 \\\\  & =0.04\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert the result to a percent:\n\n$$\n0.04=0.04\\times 100%=4%\n$$\n\nSo, the percent markup is $4%.$"] [17592186651576 :question/problem "Alina bought a house for $\\$36,040,$ including a $6%$ real estate agent's commission. What was the price of the house before the commission was added?"] [17592186651576 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percent paid}\\times \\text{original price}=\\text{final price}.\n$$\n\nThe commission was $6%,$ so the percent that Alina paid was\n\n$$\n100%+6%=106%.\n$$\n\nExpressing $106%$ as a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n106%=106÷100=1.06\\,.\n$$\n\nWe also know that the final price was $\\$36,040.$ So, if $p$ was the original price, then we have the following equation which we can solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.06p & =36,040 \\\\ p & =\\frac{36,040}{1.06} \\\\ p & =34,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original price of the house, before the commission, was $\\$34,000.$"] [17592186651580 :question/problem "Due to bad weather, some crops of flowers were destroyed. Because of the lack of supply, a florist increases the price of a bunch of tulips by $30%$ to $\\$7.15.$ What was the price of a bunch of tulips before the price hike?"] [17592186651580 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percent paid}\\times \\text{original price}=\\text{final price}.\n$$\n\nThe gratuity was $30%,$ so the percent that Nina's family paid was\n\n$$\n100%+30%=130%.\n$$\n\nExpressing $130%$ as a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n130%=130÷100=1.3\\,.\n$$\n\nWe also know that the final price was $\\$7.15.$ So, if $p$ was the original price, then we have the following equation which we can solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.3p & =7.15 \\\\ p & =\\frac{7.15}{1.3} \\\\ p & =5.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original price of a bunch of tulips, before the price hike, was $\\$5.50.$"] [17592186651585 :question/problem "Helen requested the services of a babysitter for $4$ hours. The service's total cost was $\\$28,$ including an additional $12%$ due to the babysitter working an additional hour outside of what Helen agreed to. What was the original cost of the service?"] [17592186651585 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percent paid}\\times \\text{original price}=\\text{final price}.\n$$\n\nThe additional was $12%,$ so the percent that Helen paid was\n\n$$\n100%+12%=112%.\n$$\n\nExpressing $112%$ as a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n112%=112÷100=1.12\\,.\n$$\n\nWe also know that the final price was $\\$28.$ So, if $p$ was the original price, then we have the following equation, which we can solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.12p & =28 \\\\ p & =\\frac{28}{1.12} \\\\ p & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original price of the service of the babysitter, before the additional cost, was $\\$25.00$"] [17592186651604 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percent markdown}=\\frac{\\text{amount of markdown}}{\\text{original price}}.\n$$\n\nThe original price is $525$ dollars. The amount of markdown is $525-357=168$ dollars. Substituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{percent markdown} & =\\frac{168}{525} \\\\  & =\\frac{168÷21}{525÷21} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{25} \\\\  & =8÷25 \\\\  & =0.32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert the result to a percent:\n\n$$\n0.32=0.32\\times 100%=32%\n$$\n\nTherefore, the percent markdown is $32%.$"] [17592186651609 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percent markup}=\\frac{\\text{amount of markup}}{\\text{original price}}.\n$$\n\nThe original price is $2.50$ dollars. The amount of markup is $1$ dollar. Substituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{percent markup} & =\\frac{1}{2.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 2}{2.5\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =2÷5 \\\\  & =0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert the result to a percent:\n\n$$\n0.4=0.4\\times 100%=40%\n$$\n\nSo, the percent markup is $40%.$"] [17592186651621 :question/problem "The manager of a clothes store places a pair of jeans on the $\\$10$ sale shelf. If the markdown was $80%,$ what was the original price of the jeans?"] [17592186651621 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percent paid}\\times \\text{original price}=\\text{final price}.\n$$\n\nThe markdown was $80%,$ so the percent that the jeans now cost is\n\n$$\n100%-80%=20%.\n$$\n\nExpressing $20%$ as a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n20%=20÷100=0.2\\,.\n$$\n\nWe also know that the final price was $\\$10.$ So, if $p$ was the original price, then we have the following equation which we can solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2p & =10 \\\\ p & =\\frac{10}{0.2} \\\\ p & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original price of the jeans was $\\$50.$"] [17592186651628 :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{percent markup}=\\frac{\\text{amount of markup}}{\\text{original price}}.\n$$\n\nThe original price is $680$ dollars. The amount of markup is $782-680=102$ dollars. Substituting these values into the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{percent markup} & =\\frac{102}{680} \\\\  & =\\frac{102÷34}{680÷34} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{20} \\\\  & =3÷20 \\\\  & =0.15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert the result to a percent:\n\n$$\n0.15=0.15\\times 100%=15%\n$$\n\nSo, the percent markup is $15%.$"] [17592186652234 :question/problem "A sum of $\\$800$ is invested in a bond. It earns simple interest of $7%$ per year. How much interest is earned after $4$ years?"] [17592186652234 :question/worked-solution "Simple interest is computed according to the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Simple Interest}=\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time}.\n$$\n\nHere, the principal is $\\$800$,  the rate is $7%$ per year, and the time is $4$ years. Converting the rate to a decimal, we have:\n\n$$\n7%=7÷100=0.07\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\  & =800\\times 0.07\\times 4 \\\\  & =224.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of interest earned is $\\$224.$"] [17592186652238 :question/problem "A sum of $\\$4,000$ is deposited in a bank. It earns simple interest of $5%$ per year.  How much interest is earned after $4$ years?"] [17592186652238 :question/worked-solution "Simple interest is computed according to the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Simple Interest}=\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time}.\n$$\n\nHere, the principal is $\\$4,000$,  the rate is $5%$ per year, and the time is $4$ years. Converting the rate to a decimal, we have:\n\n$$\n5%=5÷100=0.05\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\  & =4,000\\times 0.05\\times 4 \\\\  & =800.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of interest earned is $\\$800.$"] [17592186652242 :question/problem "A sum of $\\$3,200$ is deposited in the bank. It earns simple interest of $6.5%$ per year. What is the final balance after $10$ years?"] [17592186652242 :question/worked-solution "The principal is $\\$3,200,$ the rate is $6.5%$ per year, and the time is $10$ years.\n\nConverting the rate to a decimal, we have:\n\n$$\n6.5%=6.5÷100=0.065\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for simple interest, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\  & =3,200\\times 0.065\\times 10 \\\\  & =2,080.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of interest earned is $\\$2,080.$ To find the final balance, we add the interest amount to the principal:\n\n$$\n3,200+2,080=5,280\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final balance will be $\\$5,280.$"] [17592186652245 :question/problem "A sum of $\\$2,000$ is deposited in the bank. It earns simple interest of $7%$ per year. What is the final balance after $5$ years?"] [17592186652245 :question/worked-solution "The principal is $\\$2,000,$ the rate is $7%$ per year, and the time is $5$ years.\n\nConverting the rate to a decimal, we have:\n\n$$\n7%=7÷100=0.07\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for simple interest, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\  & =2,000\\times 0.07\\times 5 \\\\  & =700.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of interest earned is $\\$700.$ To find the final balance, we add the interest amount to the principal:\n\n$$\n2,000+700=2,700\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final balance will be $\\$2,700.$"] [17592186652246 :question/problem "Sarah invested $\\$4,500$ in a savings bond. It earns simple interest of $4.2%$ per year. After some years, the final balance was $\\$5,445.$ How long did Sarah hold the bond?"] [17592186652246 :question/worked-solution "Here, the principal is $\\$4,500,$ and the interest rate is $4.2%$ per year. Converting the interest rate to a decimal, we get\n\n$$\n4.2%=4.2÷100=0.042\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n5,445-4,500=945\n$$\n\nSo the amount of interest earned is $\\$945.$ \n\nLet $n$ be the number of years for which the bond was held. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 945 & =4,500\\times 0.042\\times n \\\\ 945 & =189n \\\\ n & =\\frac{945}{189} \\\\ n & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the bond was held for $5$ years."] [17592186652247 :question/worked-solution "Here, the principal is $\\$1,200,$ and the time is $8$ years. To find the interest earned, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n1,680-1,200=480\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$480.$ \n\nLet $r$ be the interest rate. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 480 & =1,200\\times r\\times 8 \\\\ 480 & =9,600r \\\\ r & =\\frac{480}{9,600} \\\\ r & =0.05\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting the rate $r=0.05$ to a percent, we get\n\n$$\n0.05=0.05\\times 100%=5%.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interest rate is $5%.$"] [17592186652249 :question/worked-solution "Here, the principal is $\\$1,750,$ and the time is $10$ years. To find the interest earned, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n3,150-1,750=1,400\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$1,400.$ \n\nLet $r$ be the interest rate. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 1,400 & =1,750\\times r\\times 10 \\\\ 1,400 & =17,500r \\\\ r & =\\frac{1,400}{17,500} \\\\ r & =0.08\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting the rate $r=0.08$ to a percent, we get\n\n$$\n0.08=0.08\\times 100%=8%.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interest rate is $8%.$"] [17592186652252 :question/problem "A sum of $\\$950$ is deposited in a bank. It earns a simple interest of $7.5%$ per year. After several years, the final balance is $\\$1,662.50.$ How long was the deposit held in the bank?"] [17592186652252 :question/worked-solution "Here, the principal is $\\$950,$ and the interest rate is $7.5%$ per year. Converting the interest rate to a decimal, we get\n\n$$\n7.5%=7.5÷100=0.075\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n1,662.50-950=712.50\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$712.50.$ \n\nLet $n$ be the number of years for which the deposit was held in the bank. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 712.50 & =950\\times 0.075\\times n \\\\ 712.50 & =71.25n \\\\ n & =\\frac{712.50}{71.25} \\\\ n & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the deposit was held in the bank for $10$ years."] [17592186652266 :question/problem "A sum of $\\$500$ is deposited in the bank. It earns simple interest of $5%$ per year. After some years, the final balance is $\\$625.$ How long was the deposit held in the bank?"] [17592186652266 :question/worked-solution "Here, the principal is $\\$500,$ and the interest rate is $5%$ per year. Converting the interest rate to a decimal, we get\n\n$$\n5%=5÷100=0.05\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n625-500=125\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$125.$ \n\nLet $n$ be the number of years for which the deposit was held in the bank. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 125 & =500\\times 0.05\\times n \\\\ 125 & =25n \\\\ n & =\\frac{125}{25} \\\\ n & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the deposit was held in the bank for $5$ years."] [17592186652268 :question/problem "Michael deposited a principal of $\\$4,000$ in his savings account where it will earn $3.5%$ simple interest per year. How much interest will Michael earn in $4$ years?"] [17592186652268 :question/worked-solution "Simple interest is computed according to the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Simple Interest}=\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time}.\n$$\n\nHere, the principal is $\\$4,000,$ the rate is $3.5%$ per year, and the time is $4$ years. Converting the rate to a decimal, we have:\n\n$$\n3.5%=3.5÷100=0.035\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\  & =4,000\\times 0.035\\times 4 \\\\  & =560.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of interest earned is $\\$560.$"] [17592186652272 :question/problem "Liam invested a principal of $\\$8,000$ in a fixed deposit account where it will earn $5.25%$ simple interest per year. What will be the final balance after $4$ years?"] [17592186652272 :question/worked-solution "Here, the principal is $\\$8,000,$ the rate is $5.25%$ per year, and the time is $4$ years. \n\nConverting the rate to a decimal, we have:\n\n$$\n5.25%=5.25÷100=0.0525\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for simple interest, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\  & =8,000\\times 0.0525\\times 4 \\\\  & =1,680.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of interest earned is $\\$1,680.$ To find the final balance, we add the interest amount to the principal:\n\n$$\n8,000+1,680=9,680\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final balance will be $\\$9,680.$"] [17592186652273 :question/problem "A sum of $\\$1,000$ is deposited in a bank. It earns simple interest of $9%$ per year.  How much interest is earned after $5$ years?"] [17592186652273 :question/worked-solution "Simple interest is computed according to the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Simple Interest}=\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time}.\n$$\n\nHere, the principal is $\\$1,000$,  the rate is $9%$ per year, and the time is $5$ years. Converting the rate to a decimal, we have:\n\n$$\n9%=9÷100=0.09\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\  & =1,000\\times 0.09\\times 5 \\\\  & =450.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of interest earned is $\\$450.$"] [17592186652276 :question/worked-solution "Here, the principal is $\\$18,500,$ and the time is $4$ years. To find the interest earned, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n24,420-18,500=5,920\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$5,920.$ \n\nLet $r$ be the interest rate. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 5,920 & =18,500\\times r\\times 4 \\\\ 5,920 & =74,000r \\\\ r & =\\frac{5,920}{74,000} \\\\ r & =0.08\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting the rate $r=0.08$ to a percent, we get\n\n$$\n0.08=0.08\\times 100%=8%.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interest rate is $8%.$"] [17592186652280 :question/problem "Bobby deposited $\\$3,400$ in his bank account. It earns simple interest of $8.8%$ per year. After some years, the final balance was $\\$4,596.80.$ How long did Bobby hold the deposit in the bank?"] [17592186652280 :question/worked-solution "Here, the principal is $\\$3,400,$ and the interest rate is $8.8%$ per year. Converting the interest rate to a decimal, we get\n\n$$\n8.8%=8.8÷100=0.088\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n4,596.80-3,400=1,196.80\n$$\n\nSo the amount of interest earned is $\\$1,196.80.$ \n\nLet $n$ be the number of years for which the deposit was held in the bank. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 1,196.80 & =3,400\\times 0.088\\times n \\\\ 1,196.80 & =299.20n \\\\ n & =\\frac{1,196.80}{299.20} \\\\ n & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the deposit was held in the bank for $4$ years."] [17592186652281 :question/problem "Charles invested a principal of $\\$1,800$ in his bank account where he will earn $6.3%$ \nsimple interest per year. How much interest will Charles earn in $8$ years?"] [17592186652281 :question/worked-solution "Simple interest is computed according to the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Simple Interest}=\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time}.\n$$\n\nHere, the principal is $\\$1,800$,  the rate is $6.3%$ per year, and the time is $8$ years. Converting the rate to a decimal, we have:\n\n$$\n6.3%=6.3÷100=0.063\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\  & =1,800\\times 0.063\\times 8 \\\\  & =907.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of interest earned is $\\$907.20.$"] [17592186652298 :question/problem "Nick deposited a principal of $\\$5,000$ in his bank account where it will earn $7.5%$ simple interest per year. What will be the final balance after $5$ years?"] [17592186652298 :question/worked-solution "Here, the principal is $\\$5,000,$ the rate is $7.5%$ per year, and the time is $5$ years. \n\nConverting the rate to a decimal, we have:\n\n$$\n7.5%=7.5÷100=0.075\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for simple interest, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\  & =5,000\\times 0.075\\times 5 \\\\  & =1,875.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of interest earned is $\\$1,875.$ To find the final balance, we add the interest amount to the principal:\n\n$$\n5,000+1,875=6,875\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final balance will be $\\$6,875.$"] [17592186652300 :question/problem "Liam deposited $\\$7,200$ in a fixed deposit account. It earns simple interest of $3.5%$ per year. After some years, the final balance was $\\$8,208.$ How long did Liam keep the money in the account?"] [17592186652300 :question/worked-solution "Here, the principal is $\\$7,200,$ and the interest rate is $3.5%$ per year. Converting the interest rate to a decimal, we get\n\n$$\n3.5%=3.5÷100=0.035\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n8,208-7,200=1,008\n$$\n\nSo the amount of interest earned is $\\$1,008.$ \n\nLet $n$ be the number of years for which the money was kept in the account. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 1,008 & =7,200\\times 0.035\\times n \\\\ 1,008 & =252n \\\\ n & =\\frac{1,008}{252} \\\\ n & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the money was kept in the account for $4$ years."] [17592186652306 :question/problem "Louis deposited a principal of $\\$3,500$ in his bank account where it will earn $9%$ simple interest per year. What will be the final balance after $12$ years?"] [17592186652306 :question/worked-solution "Here, the principal is $\\$3,500,$ the rate is $9%$ per year, and the time is $12$ years. \n\nConverting the rate to a decimal, we have:\n\n$$\n9%=9÷100=0.09\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for simple interest, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\  & =3,500\\times 0.09\\times 12 \\\\  & =3,780.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of interest earned is $\\$3,780.$ To find the final balance, we add the interest amount to the principal:\n\n$$\n3,500+3,780=7,280\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final balance will be $\\$7,280.$"] [17592186652309 :question/worked-solution "The principal is $\\$3,100,$ and the time is $6$ years. To find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n4,495-3,100=1,395\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$1,395.$ \n\nLet $r$ be the interest rate. Using the formula, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 1,395 & =3,100\\times r\\times 6 \\\\ 1,395 & =18,600r \\\\ r & =\\frac{1395}{18,600} \\\\ r & =0.075\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting the rate $r=0.075$ to a percent, we get\n\n$$\n0.075=0.075\\times 100%=7.5%.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interest rate is $7.5%.$"] [17592186652318 :question/worked-solution "The principal is $\\$5,000,$ and the time is $6$ years. To find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n6,050-5,000=1,050\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$1,050.$ \n\nLet $r$ be the interest rate. Using the formula, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 1,050 & =5,000\\times r\\times 6 \\\\ 1,050 & =30,000r \\\\ r & =\\frac{1,050}{30,000} \\\\ r & =0.035\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting the rate $r=0.035$ to a percent, we get\n\n$$\n0.035=0.035\\times 100%=3.5%.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interest rate is $3.5%.$"] [17592186652912 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=25$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $80$ and the sample standard deviation is $6,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.797)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $24$ degrees of freedom.*"] [17592186652912 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=80,\\,n=25,\\,s=6.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.797)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{24,0.005}=2.797.\n$$\n\n![](images/4f/4f9a0b7c9ec55a5c061f709b2d6fa02c3f760ccd66faab24c1201c4cf48bb18c.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 80\\pm 2.797\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & 80\\pm 3.356\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186652959 :question/problem "![](images/52/52f02ef08e5368a71a0dab8c28266091c7235b42dcc03f23b83d59297c111635.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=16$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $64,$ and the sample standard deviation is $5,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.131)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $15$ degrees of freedom,  as shown in the diagram above.*"] [17592186652959 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=64,\\,n=16,\\,s=5.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.131)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.025}=2.131.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64 & \\pm 2.131\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 64 & \\pm 2.664\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186654749 :question/problem "Suppose each call to a popular radio station has a $2%$ chance of being answered. On average, how many calls must be made to the radio station in order to be answered?"] [17592186654793 :question/problem "An archer practices shooting arrows at a target. The probability that the archer hits the bullseye is $35%.$ \nWhat is the variance in the number of shots made by the archer before hitting the bullseye? Round your answer to $1$ decimal place."] [17592186656180 :question/problem "A study has determined how the willingness of students in a college math class to attend or not attend the next class changes once they attend or are absent from the previous class. The following table describes the results of this study. \n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Attend</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Absent</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Attend</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Absent</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThat is, $10%$ of the students who attend a class will be absent in the next class, while $70%$ of the students who were absent from a class will attend the next class. \n\nAssuming that the study was conducted on a total of  $240$ students and that the given probabilities remained constant over time, determine the number of students who regularly attended math class."] [17592186662277 :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2},{\\sigma }_{4}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(11)^{2}+(6)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =177\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the geneticist used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(11)^{2}+(6)^{2} \\\\  & =157.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{157}{177} \\\\  & ≈0.887 \\\\  & =88.7%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186662284 :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=36$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =7.5.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=13,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*"] [17592186662284 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{7.5}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\ 13\\pm 2.056\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186662287 :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{4}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2},{\\sigma }_{4}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(8)^{2}+(6)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =120\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the engineer used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(8)^{2} \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{64}{120} \\\\  & ≈0.533 \\\\  & =53.3%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186662326 :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(4)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =57.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the trainer used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(6)^{2}+(4)^{2} \\\\  & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{52}{57.01} \\\\  & ≈0.912 \\\\  & =91.2%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186662340 :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2},{\\sigma }_{4}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},{y}_{4}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(10)^{2}+(9)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =194\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the biologist used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(10)^{2}+(9)^{2} \\\\  & =181.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{181}{194} \\\\  & ≈0.933 \\\\  & =93.3%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186663387 :question/worked-solution "We can write the given problem as the following product:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,%\\times 5=9\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,%=9÷5=\\frac{9}{5}\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we write an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{5}=\\frac{9\\times 20}{5\\times 20}=\\frac{180}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{180}{100}=180%\n$$"] [17592186663391 :question/worked-solution "Using the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,%=6÷24=\\frac{6}{24}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{24}=\\frac{6÷6}{24÷6}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we write an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1\\times 25}{4\\times 25}=\\frac{25}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{100}=25%\n$$"] [17592186663393 :question/worked-solution "We can write the given problem as the following product:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,%\\times 10=3\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,%=3÷10=\\frac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we write an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{3\\times 10}{10\\times 10}=\\frac{30}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{100}=30%\n$$"] [17592186663405 :question/worked-solution "We can write the given problem as the following product:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,%\\times 5=1\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,%=1÷5=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we write an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1\\times 20}{5\\times 20}=\\frac{20}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{100}=20%\n$$"] [17592186664067 :question/worked-solution "The shaded part in the picture shows $\\frac{1}{5}$ of a whole.\n\nNow, we want to get an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $100.$ So, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $20:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{1\\times 20}{5\\times 20}=\\frac{20}{100}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{20}{100}$ is equivalent to $20%.$"] [17592186664081 :question/worked-solution "The shaded part in the picture shows $\\frac{9}{20}$ of a whole.\n\nNow, we want to get an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $100.$ So, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{20}=\\frac{9\\times 5}{20\\times 5}=\\frac{45}{100}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{45}{100}$ is equivalent to $45%.$"] [17592186664089 :question/problem "Which model represents $50%?$"] [17592186664089 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that $50%$ is the same as $\\frac{50}{100}.$\n\nWe now need to simplify this fraction. Dividing the numerator and denominator by $10,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{50}{100}=\\frac{50÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{5}{10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{10}=\\frac{5÷5}{10÷5}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe model that shows $\\frac{1}{2}$ is:\n\n![](images/63/63471e31db02dd306aeb7d24009ec4f86d6f5f1d14c579883b0502113d839a98.png)"] [17592186664104 :question/problem "If a circle represents a whole, which model shows $120%?$"] [17592186664104 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that $120%$ is the same as $\\frac{120}{100}.$\n\nWe now need to simplify this fraction. Dividing the numerator and denominator by $10,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{120}{100}=\\frac{120÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{12}{10}.\n$$\n\nNext, we divide the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{10}=\\frac{12÷2}{10÷2}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write $\\frac{6}{5}$ as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{5}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nThe model that shows $1\\,\\frac{1}{5}$ is:\n\n![](images/ab/abb533b86988c58e1c2504137a024f073e941c5f8f97aa919dd98a0acb33946d.png)"] [17592186664112 :question/worked-solution "One large square representing one whole is divided into $100$ equal tiles.\n\nWe have $1$ fully shaded large square ($100$ shaded tiles). Additionally, we have $80$ shaded tiles in the second large square. Hence, there are $180$ shaded tiles in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents $180%.$"] [17592186664788 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0.87 & 0.77 & 0.74 & -1.54 \\\\ 0.52 & 0.44 & -1.28 & 0.15 \\\\ -0.58 & 0.85 & 1.21 & 1.13 \\\\ 0.67 & -1.45 & -0.59 & 0.53 \\\\ -1.48 & -0.62 & -0.07 & -0.26\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.586 & -0.592 & -0.244 & 0.213 & 0.448 \\\\ -0.244 & -0.313 & 0.395 & -0.695 & 0.451 \\\\ 0.425 & 0.609 & 0.481 & 0.139 & 0.445 \\\\ -0.63 & -0.11 & 0.106 & 0.618 & 0.444 \\\\ -0.139 & 0.41 & -0.736 & -0.264 & 0.448\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.486 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.33 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.664 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1.282 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.032 & -0.735 & 0.526 & 0.427 \\\\ 0.686 & -0.074 & 0.419 & -0.59 \\\\ 0.66 & 0.315 & -0.07 & 0.678 \\\\ -0.305 & 0.596 & 0.737 & 0.095\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $85%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186664788 :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=2.486,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2.33,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.664,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=1.282.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.486}^{2}}{{2.486}^{2}+{2.33}^{2}+{1.664}^{2}+{1.282}^{2}}≈38.6%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.486}^{2}+{2.33}^{2}}{{2.486}^{2}+{2.33}^{2}+{1.664}^{2}+{1.282}^{2}}≈72.5%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first three principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.486}^{2}+{2.33}^{2}+{1.664}^{2}}{{2.486}^{2}+{2.33}^{2}+{1.664}^{2}+{1.282}^{2}}≈89.7%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first four principal components is $100%.$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $38.6%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $72.5%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $3$ dimensions would preserve $89.7%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $4$ dimensions would preserve $100%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/b1/b1c919de687c5e35fd3a13bf092b2f2257beb779f11839a09d019cd4f6192b75.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $3\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $85%$ of the variance."] [17592186664790 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0.91 & -0.89 & -0.9 & -1.61 \\\\ 0.65 & 1.42 & -0.1 & -0.13 \\\\ -1.28 & 0.44 & -0.52 & 0.98 \\\\ 0.59 & 0.04 & 1.7 & 0.12 \\\\ -0.87 & -1.01 & -0.18 & 0.64\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.726 & 0.465 & 0.033 & 0.237 & 0.447 \\\\ -0.12 & -0.438 & 0.633 & -0.44 & 0.447 \\\\ 0.589 & 0.178 & 0.314 & 0.568 & 0.447 \\\\ -0.07 & -0.622 & -0.591 & 0.242 & 0.447 \\\\ 0.326 & 0.417 & -0.388 & -0.608 & 0.447\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.711 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.303 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.826 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.192 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.671 & -0.356 & 0.015 & -0.651 \\\\ 0.148 & -0.609 & 0.753 & 0.198 \\\\ 0.067 & -0.694 & -0.652 & 0.296 \\\\ 0.724 & -0.141 & -0.08 & -0.671\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186664790 :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=2.711,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2.303,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.826,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.192.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.711}^{2}}{{2.711}^{2}+{2.303}^{2}+{1.826}^{2}+{0.192}^{2}}≈45.9%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.711}^{2}+{2.303}^{2}}{{2.711}^{2}+{2.303}^{2}+{1.826}^{2}+{0.192}^{2}}≈79.0%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first three principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.711}^{2}+{2.303}^{2}+{1.826}^{2}}{{2.711}^{2}+{2.303}^{2}+{1.826}^{2}+{0.192}^{2}}≈99.8%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $45.9%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $79.0%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $3$ dimensions would preserve $99.8%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/c6/c62d178d5e6493010dcc469e15aa7cc36d4487705e4492db8ccc3b6b9b09806d.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $3\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance."] [17592186664800 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1.318 & 1.348 & -1.256 \\\\ -2.145 & -4.683 & -2.801 \\\\ -1.408 & -1.649 & -3.270 \\\\ 0.084 & -1.661 & -2.349 \\\\ 0.334 & 0.207 & -2.306\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe wish to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset while still preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning that walks through the process of reducing the dimensionality of the dataset.\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is \n{{field-1}} \nwith the \n{{field-2}} \nvariance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction in feature space \n{{field-3}} \nto the first\n{{field-4}}. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to {{field-5}}.\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1.21 & 1.15 & 1.52 \\\\ -1.28 & -1.48 & -0.54 \\\\ -0.75 & -0.16 & -1.17 \\\\ 0.32 & -0.16 & 0.06 \\\\ 0.50 & 0.65 & 0.12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.684 & 0.273 & -0.412 & 0.534 & 0.053 \\\\ 0.587 & 0.604 & -0.222 & 0.229 & 0.434 \\\\ 0.366 & -0.657 & -0.270 & 0.600 & -0.037 \\\\ -0.042 & 0.114 & 0.838 & 0.530 & 0.047 \\\\ -0.226 & -0.341 & 0.077 & -0.145 & 0.897\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3.265 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.093 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.372 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.607 & -0.077 & 0.791 \\\\ -0.568 & -0.654 & -0.500 \\\\ -0.556 & 0.753 & -0.353\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n{{field-6}}.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n{{field-7}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-8}}.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={{field-9}}.\n$$"] [17592186664800 :question/worked-solution "We will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is $\\text{the direction in feature space}$ with the $\\text{most}$ variance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction $\\text{orthogonal}$ to the first $\\text{principal component}.$\n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to $\\text{standardize the data}.$\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1.21 & 1.15 & 1.52 \\\\ -1.28 & -1.48 & -0.54 \\\\ -0.75 & -0.16 & -1.17 \\\\ 0.32 & -0.16 & 0.06 \\\\ 0.50 & 0.65 & 0.12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.684 & 0.273 & -0.412 & 0.534 & 0.053 \\\\ 0.587 & 0.604 & -0.222 & 0.229 & 0.434 \\\\ 0.366 & -0.657 & -0.270 & 0.600 & -0.037 \\\\ -0.042 & 0.114 & 0.838 & 0.530 & 0.047 \\\\ -0.226 & -0.341 & 0.077 & -0.145 & 0.897\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3.265 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.093 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.372 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.607 & -0.077 & 0.791 \\\\ -0.568 & -0.654 & -0.500 \\\\ -0.556 & 0.753 & -0.353\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{3.265}^{2}}{{3.265}^{2}+{1.093}^{2}+{0.372}^{2}}≈88.9%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{3.265}^{2}+{1.093}^{2}}{{3.265}^{2}+{1.093}^{2}+{0.372}^{2}}≈98.8%.\n$$\n\nThus, we have that \n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $88.9%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $98.8%$ of the variance.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions}$ , since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={X}_{\\text{std}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} & {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nsince we need to project the data onto the first two principal components, which are the first two columns of $V.$\n\n**Note:** In order to invert the projection and interpret any further analysis in terms of the original features, we need to know the means and standard deviations of the original dataset $X.$ Additionally, in case we have more data, we can use the means and standard deviations of the original dataset to map the new data into the original feature space."] [17592186664821 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-4.308 & 4.114 & -0.030 \\\\ -4.553 & 4.571 & -1.219 \\\\ -4.236 & 0.827 & -0.561 \\\\ -1.455 & 7.633 & 1.796 \\\\ -3.558 & 4.254 & -1.067\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe wish to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset while still preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning that walks through the process of reducing the dimensionality of the dataset.\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is \n{{field-1}} \nwith the \n{{field-2}} \nvariance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction in feature space \n{{field-3}} \nto the first\n{{field-4}}. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to {{field-5}}.\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.54 & -0.07 & 0.15 \\\\ -0.74 & 0.12 & -0.82 \\\\ -0.48 & -1.43 & -0.28 \\\\ 1.71 & 1.39 & 1.65 \\\\ 0.05 & -0.01 & -0.70\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.088 & 0.029 & -0.711 & 0.377 & 0.586 \\\\ -0.270 & -0.579 & -0.163 & 0.519 & -0.544 \\\\ -0.390 & 0.745 & 0.168 & 0.475 & -0.196 \\\\ 0.868 & 0.115 & 0.045 & 0.466 & -0.121 \\\\ -0.120 & -0.309 & 0.661 & 0.381 & 0.555\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3.169 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.209 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.690 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.603 & 0.196 & 0.773 \\\\ 0.549 & -0.805 & -0.224 \\\\ 0.579 & 0.560 & -0.593\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n{{field-6}}.\n\nThe fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n{{field-7}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-8}}.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={{field-9}}.\n$$"] [17592186664821 :question/worked-solution "We will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is $\\text{the direction in feature space}$ with the $\\text{most}$ variance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction $\\text{orthogonal}$ to the first $\\text{principal component}.$\n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to $\\text{standardize the data}.$\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{std}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.54 & -0.07 & 0.15 \\\\ -0.74 & 0.12 & -0.82 \\\\ -0.48 & -1.43 & -0.28 \\\\ 1.71 & 1.39 & 1.65 \\\\ 0.05 & -0.01 & -0.70\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.088 & 0.029 & -0.711 & 0.377 & 0.586 \\\\ -0.270 & -0.579 & -0.163 & 0.519 & -0.544 \\\\ -0.390 & 0.745 & 0.168 & 0.475 & -0.196 \\\\ 0.868 & 0.115 & 0.045 & 0.466 & -0.121 \\\\ -0.120 & -0.309 & 0.661 & 0.381 & 0.555\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3.169 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.209 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.690 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.603 & 0.196 & 0.773 \\\\ 0.549 & -0.805 & -0.224 \\\\ 0.579 & 0.560 & -0.593\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{3.169}^{2}}{{3.169}^{2}+{1.209}^{2}+{0.690}^{2}}≈83.8%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{3.169}^{2}+{1.209}^{2}}{{3.169}^{2}+{1.209}^{2}+{0.690}^{2}}≈96.0%.\n$$\n\nThus, we have that \n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $83.8%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $96.0%$ of the variance.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions}$ , since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance.\n\nWriting ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ for the columns of $V$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3},{u}_{4},{u}_{5}$ for the columns of $U$, the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\n{X}_{\\text{red}}={X}_{\\text{std}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} & {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nsince we need to project the data onto the first two principal components, which are the first two columns of $V.$\n\n**Note:** In order to invert the projection and interpret any further analysis in terms of the original features, we need to know the means and standard deviations of the original dataset $X.$ Additionally, in case we have more data, we can use the means and standard deviations of the original dataset to map the new data into the original feature space."] [17592186664827 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1.21 & 1.15 & 1.52 \\\\ -1.28 & -1.48 & -0.54 \\\\ -0.75 & -0.16 & -1.17 \\\\ 0.32 & -0.16 & 0.06 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.65 & 0.12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.684 & 0.273 & -0.412 & 0.534 & 0.053 \\\\ 0.587 & 0.604 & -0.222 & 0.229 & 0.434 \\\\ 0.366 & -0.657 & -0.27 & 0.6 & -0.037 \\\\ -0.042 & 0.114 & 0.838 & 0.53 & 0.047 \\\\ -0.226 & -0.341 & 0.077 & -0.145 & 0.897\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}3.265 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.093 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.372 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.607 & -0.077 & 0.791 \\\\ -0.568 & -0.654 & -0.5 \\\\ -0.556 & 0.753 & -0.353\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $85%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186664827 :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.265,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.093,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=0.372.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.265}^{2}}{{3.265}^{2}+{1.093}^{2}+{0.372}^{2}}≈88.9%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.265}^{2}+{1.093}^{2}}{{3.265}^{2}+{1.093}^{2}+{0.372}^{2}}≈98.8%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $88.9%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $98.8%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/b2/b2a8e5441c5355de0ef1a2c50a921f17f7ec4b62579d7db685459889fa6ada62.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $1\\text{ dimension},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $85%$ of the variance."] [17592186664830 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1.09 & 0.7 & -0.57 \\\\ -0.46 & -0.17 & -0.42 \\\\ -1.08 & -1.21 & -1 \\\\ 1.04 & 1.29 & 0.5 \\\\ -0.59 & -0.6 & 1.5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.41 & -0.396 & 0.618 & -0.214 & 0.497 \\\\ 0.18 & -0.166 & -0.607 & -0.526 & 0.544 \\\\ 0.624 & -0.368 & 0.096 & 0.584 & 0.353 \\\\ -0.606 & 0.12 & -0.404 & 0.579 & 0.347 \\\\ 0.208 & 0.816 & 0.279 & -0.032 & 0.461\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.82 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.977 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.378 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.694 & -0.159 & 0.702 \\\\ -0.702 & -0.07 & -0.709 \\\\ -0.162 & 0.985 & 0.063\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}."] [17592186664830 :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=2.82,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.977,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=0.378.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.82}^{2}}{{2.82}^{2}+{1.977}^{2}+{0.378}^{2}}≈66.2%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.82}^{2}+{1.977}^{2}}{{2.82}^{2}+{1.977}^{2}+{0.378}^{2}}≈98.8%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $66.2%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $98.8%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/72/724b328795c44214650622f8fb6cdf28b8b56a9b6033b75434da6e83f373f10c.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance."] [17592186670007 :question/worked-solution "The principal is $\\$2,000,$ and the time is $4$ years. To find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n2,480-2,000=480\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$480.$ \n\nLet $r$ be the interest rate. Using the formula, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 480 & =2,000\\times r\\times 4 \\\\ 480 & =8,000r \\\\ r & =\\frac{480}{8,000} \\\\ r & =0.06\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting the rate $r=0.06$ to a percent, we get\n\n$$\n0.06=0.06\\times 100%=6%.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interest rate is $6%.$"] [17592186670013 :question/problem "Kathryn answered $88%$ of the questions correctly on her last trigonometry test. What fraction of questions did she answer correctly?"] [17592186670013 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign $(88)$ as a fraction with denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{88}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{88}{100}=\\frac{88÷4}{100÷4}=\\frac{22}{25}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $88%=\\frac{22}{25}.$"] [17592186670024 :question/problem "Express $5%$ as a fraction in its simplest form.\n\n$5%=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186670024 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign ($5$) as a fraction with denominator $100$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{100}\n$$\n\nWe now simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{100}=\\frac{5÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{1}{20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5%=\\frac{1}{20}.$"] [17592186670029 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{7}{25}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{25}=\\frac{7\\times 4}{25\\times 4}=\\frac{28}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{28}{100}=28%\n$$"] [17592186670038 :question/problem "A sum of $\\$2,000$ is deposited in a bank. It earns simple interest of $5%$ per year. After several years, the final balance is $\\$2,400.$ How long was the deposit held in the bank?"] [17592186670038 :question/worked-solution "Here, the principal is $\\$2,000,$ and the interest rate is $5%$ per year. Converting the interest rate to a decimal, we get\n\n$$\n5%=5÷100=0.05\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n2,400-2,000=400\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$400.$ \n\nLet $n$ be the number of years for which the deposit was held in the bank. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 400 & =2,000\\times 0.05\\times n \\\\ 400 & =100n \\\\ n & =\\frac{400}{100} \\\\ n & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the deposit was held in the bank for $4$ years."] [17592186670041 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{23}{50}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{23}{50}=\\frac{23\\times 2}{50\\times 2}=\\frac{46}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{46}{100}=46%\n$$"] [17592186670043 :question/problem "Jamie deposited a principal of $\\$4,000$ in his bank account where it will earn $8.5%$ simple interest per year. What will be the final balance after $8$ years?"] [17592186670043 :question/worked-solution "Here, the principal is $\\$4,000,$ the rate is $8.5%$ per year, and the time is $8$ years. \n\nConverting the rate to a decimal, we have:\n\n$$\n8.5%=8.5÷100=0.085\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for simple interest, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\  & =4,000\\times 0.085\\times 8 \\\\  & =2,720.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of interest earned is $\\$2,720.$ To find the final balance, we add the interest amount to the principal:\n\n$$\n4,000+2,720=6,720\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final balance will be $\\$6,720.$"] [17592186670069 :question/worked-solution "The principal is $\\$2,000,$ and the time is $8$ years. To find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n3,280-2,000=1,280\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$1,280.$ \n\nLet $r$ be the interest rate. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 1,280 & =2,000\\times r\\times 8 \\\\ 1,280 & =16,000r \\\\ r & =\\frac{1,280}{16,000} \\\\ r & =0.08\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting the rate $r=0.08$ to a percent, we get\n\n$$\n0.08=0.08\\times 100%=8%.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interest rate is $8%.$"] [17592186670084 :question/problem "Mitch deposited $\\$6,000$ in his bank account. It earns simple interest of $8.5%$ per year. After some years, the final balance was $\\$8,550.$ How long did Mitch hold the deposit in the bank?"] [17592186670084 :question/worked-solution "Here, the principal is $\\$6,000,$ and the interest rate is $8.5%$ per year. Converting the interest rate to a decimal, we get\n\n$$\n8.5%=8.5÷100=0.085\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n8,550-6,000=2,550\n$$\n\nSo the amount of interest earned is $\\$2,550.$ \n\nLet $n$ be the number of years for which the deposit was held in the bank. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 2,550 & =6,000\\times 0.085\\times n \\\\ 2,550 & =510n \\\\ n & =\\frac{2,550}{510} \\\\ n & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the deposit was held in the bank for $5$ years."] [17592186670321 :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a Bernoulli distribution?\n\nI. Recording whether a soccer player scores a penalty\n\nII. The number of victories a chess player obtains in $4$ matches given that the probability of the player winning any individual game is $60%$\n\nIII. Recording whether a randomly chosen person in a population has blood type A$-$"] [17592186670323 :question/problem "It is known that $7%$ of a population has blood type O$-.$ What is the probability that the next donor in a blood center has blood type O$-?$ Assume donors are chosen at random from the population, and the situation can be modeled as a Bernoulli trial."] [17592186670323 :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ represent the donor's blood type: $X=1$ if the donor has blood type O$-$ and $X=0$ if the donor hasn't. We wish to compute $P(X=1).$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that the donor has blood type O$-$ with probability $p=7%=0.07.$ So, we have $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.07).$\n\nIf $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.07),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.07, & x=1 \\\\ 0.93, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next donor has blood type O$-$ is\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=f(1)=0.07.\n$$"] [17592186670325 :question/problem "The final of a soccer tournament goes to \"sudden death\" penalties. The first player to score a penalty wins. A player that has scored $55%$ of his previous penalties steps up to take a penalty. Let $X$ represent the outcome of the penalty: $X=1$ if the player scores and $X=0$ if he does not. By modeling the situation as a Bernoulli trial, determine the expected value of $X.$"] [17592186670325 :question/worked-solution "From the problem statement, we know that the probability that the player will score is $p=55%=0.55.$ So, we have $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.55).$ \n\nWe wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ In general, if $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=p.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=0.55.\n$$"] [17592186670328 :question/problem "The final of a soccer tournament goes to \"sudden death\" penalties. The first player to score a penalty wins. A player that has scored $42%$ of his previous penalties steps up to take a penalty. Let $X$ represent the outcome of the penalty: $X=1$ if the player scores and $X=0$ if he does not. By modeling the situation as a Bernoulli trial, determine the standard deviation of $X.$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186670328 :question/worked-solution "From the problem statement, we know that the player scores the penalty with probability $p=42%=0.42.$ So, we have $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.42).$ \n\nWe wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$ In general, if $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{p(1-p)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{(0.42)(0.58)}≈0.494\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."]}
